    The role of the Chubby lock service in Bigtable is to keep track of tablet servers and manage their assignments. According to **5.2 Tablet Assignment (Pages 5-5)**, Bigtable uses Chubby to monitor the set of live tablet servers and their assignments. Chubby provides an efficient mechanism for tablet servers to check if they still hold their lock without incurring network traffic. The master uses Chubby to detect when a tablet server is no longer serving its tablets and to reassign those tablets as soon as possible. Additionally, Chubby is used by the master to discover the current tablet assignments at startup and to prevent concurrent master instantiations by grabbing a unique master lock. 
    
    In particular, Chubby is used for the following purposes: 
    - to keep track of tablet servers by having each tablet server create and acquire an exclusive lock on a uniquely-named file in a specific Chubby directory (**5.2 Tablet Assignment, Pages 5-5**);
    - to detect when a tablet server is no longer serving its tablets by periodically asking each tablet server for the status of its lock (**5.2 Tablet Assignment, Pages 5-5**);
    - to prevent concurrent master instantiations by having the master grab a unique master lock in Chubby (**5.2 Tablet Assignment, Pages 5-5**). 
    
    Overall, Chubby plays a crucial role in managing the assignment of tablets to tablet servers and ensuring the consistency and availability of the Bigtable system.
